      "narrative": "Maricopa County’s median sales price fell 2% year over year to $500,000 in September, while the average price rose 1.2% to $686,802, indicating stronger activity at the higher end.[10] Inventory increased 6.4% to 15,422 homes, and supply rose to 3.9 months from 3.7 months a year earlier.[10] Buyers purchased 3,702 homes, down 5.3% year over year, while pending sales fell 39.3%; new listings also declined 1.2% to 5,502, giving buyers more leverage as sellers adjust to affordability and mortgage-rate pressures.[10] Locally, semiconductor and data-center investment continues to support advanced-manufacturing and technology employment, while slower migration and population growth are tempering demand.[5]",
